meta {
  name: Fetch the notification sender keys to pin (GET /v1/notifications/sender-keys)
  type: http
  seq: 137
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/sender-keys
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{sessionToken}}
}

vars:pre-request {
  baseUrl: http://localhost:8080
}

docs {
  # GET /v1/notifications/sender-keys

  The **re-pin surface**: every sender key this instance's notifications may be verified
  against. Authenticate with the account's access token.

  Notification payloads are sealed with HPKE in `mode_auth`, so opening one on the device also
  proves it came from this instance — but only if the device holds the right public key. The
  app fetches this set on every successful Custos contact and re-pins it; that cadence is what
  keeps the window short when a key is revoked.

  ## Response (200)

  ```json
  {
    "keys": [
      { "kid": 3, "publicKey": "did:key:zDnae..." },
      { "kid": 2, "publicKey": "did:key:zDnae..." }
    ]
  }
  ```

  Newest first. Each sealed payload's envelope names the `kid` that sealed it.

  ## Rotation semantics

  - **Active** — the newest key; seals every outgoing payload. Published.
  - **Retired** (`pds notification-keys retire <kid>`) — no longer seals, still published, so a
    payload sealed just before a rotation still verifies on a device that has already re-pinned.
  - **Revoked** (`pds notification-keys revoke <kid>`) — the compromise path: gone from this set
    immediately. The next fetch is the last time a device will trust it.

  An operator rotates with `pds notification-keys rotate` (publishes a new key alongside the
  old), and retires the previous key once devices have had time to re-pin.

  ## Errors

  - `401` — missing/invalid access token
  - `501` — this instance has no `[notifications] relay` configured
  - `503` — a relay *is* configured but no usable sender key can be served: either no
    signing-key master key is set, or the stored key material will not decrypt under it (a
    backup restored across a KEK rotation, say). Deliberately an error rather than an empty
    set — a client that pinned nothing would fail to verify every notification it later
    received, with nothing to tell it why. This response never carries a `keys` array
}
